WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.120
Film Courage: What would you recommend to
a screenwriter who doesn’t have an agent

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.109
manager or producer to guide them?

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:13.440
Mark Heidelberger, Producer: So yeah…presuming
that they are up and coming, I think getting

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:20.230
an agent or a manager (not both) when you
are starting off is a great avenue because

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:24.679
you really need an advocate because a lot
of writers are not necessarily good at pitching

00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:25.679
themselves.

00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:28.769
And even if you are, there is something that
just comes off pretentious and arrogant about

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:30.359
talking about how great your work is.

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:35.690
I mean, you do the writing, let them do the
talking.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:39.890
So I do think a good agent or a manager, I
just don’t think your career is big enough,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.260
even big writers don’t often need both.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:50.790
But an advocate to go out and push for you
is always worthwhile.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.700
So assuming you don’t know anybody, I think
a good way to get to an agent or manager (a

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.760
few good ways) 1.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.960
Networking (obviously) but not necessarily
directly with agents or managers, as you get

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.380
to know more people in this industry whether
it’s producers, DP’s, actors, whoever,

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.799
you start meeting other people who are represented.

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:12.479
And a good way to get in with agents or managers
(even better than just meeting them at the

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:14.290
party and saying “Hey, look!

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:15.819
I’m a writer.

00:01:15.819 --> 00:01:20.200
Can you take a look at my stuff?”) is knowing
somebody who already knows them is often times

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.200
a better way.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.450
Instead of trying to be your own agent or
manager, look at your own resource-base and

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.549
see other people you know and “My friend
is repped by Innovative Artists.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:33.969
Hey, I’ll take you out to dinner if you
can get me…if you can just get my script

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:35.929
over and get somebody to look at it.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:37.499
Get me a meeting and I’ll take you out for
a nice dinner.”

00:01:37.499 --> 00:01:38.499
Or whatever.

00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:41.729
Leverage your own contacts because this is
a relationship business.

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:44.710
So even more than being able to go up to an
agent and say “You don’t know me, but

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.459
I’d really love for you to take a look at
my stuff.”

00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:47.939
They usually don’t.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.380
But if this comes from someone they already
know and trust, that’s a good way…so look

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.380
at who you know.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.780
That’s what I advise.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.179
I have a friend of mine (a director).

00:01:56.179 --> 00:01:58.960
He’s a visual effects guy.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:01:59.960
His name is Lloyd Barnett.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.270
He’s an amazing VFX guy.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:05.929
He was the lead compositor on AVATAR and he
directed a movie I produced called NINJA APOCALYPSE.

00:02:05.929 --> 00:02:10.209
He also did a lot of VFX in that movie.

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:11.209
And he wants to direct.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:13.530
He wants to do more directing, so I helped
him put together a director reel.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.670
And he was asking “How should I get to these
agents?”

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.860
And I’m like “You’ve been in this business
a long time.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.819
You have to know people who have agents.”

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:29.750
Think about other VFX artists that you’ve
worked with, think about other below-the-line

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.110
people you’ve worked with, composers, you
have a friend who does music… and yeah,

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.569
he’s repped by Gersh [The Gersh Agency],
talk to him.

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:40.890
See if you can get your reel looked at.”

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:45.820
I think another way, talent rises to the top,
if you believe that (I do believe that).

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.980
I believe that talent will eventually be rewarded
(real talent).

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:54.829
Unfortunately, I think a lot of people overestimate
their abilities a lot.

00:02:54.829 --> 00:03:02.049
So I think you being recognized as talented,
its got to be from someone else other than

00:03:02.049 --> 00:03:03.489
yourself saying that.

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:08.349
Other people need to recognize that talent.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:17.161
That’s what actually elevates you to the
point of…when you have other people in the

00:03:17.161 --> 00:03:20.739
industry with some modicum of success saying
“I think this guy is talented.”

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:22.819
I think that is legitimate.

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:24.810
It’s great that you believe in yourself.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.659
Having confidence is fantastic.

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:32.379
I think the whole Self-Esteem Movement has
done more to harm young kids than help them.

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:36.490
Self-Esteem is something where you believe
you are owed something.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:40.459
That you should feel good because you are
unique and you are this and you are that (as

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:46.390
opposed to have accomplished something).

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:51.219
As opposed to confidence is a different thing,
believing you can go out as opposed to self-esteem

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:56.060
and that you should be owed something just
because you’re unique, I don’t buy that.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:01.260
But I believe that you should be confident
in your abilities to a degree.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.659
Not over-confident, but confident in yourself
either that you have some modicum of talent

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:09.260
or that you have talent that you can develop
into a skill.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:15.250
And so I think some people that are good to
judge that are some of the screenplay competitions.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:22.720
And I had written a whole article on this
at FundsForWriters.com, I’d written a whole

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.030
article on screenplay competitions.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:30.880
But some of the top ones like the Sundance
Screenplay lab, like the Nichols [Fellowship],

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:37.390
Boston, Blue Cat, The Tracking Bee, Final
Draft’s Big Break, there’s a few and I

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:40.960
think they’ve elevated themselves because
they’ve been around for a long time and

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.630
they use a lot of working industry professionals
to read the scripts and grade them.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:49.090
So you have a lot of people who are in the
industry, who are in the know.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:53.889
They are not just hiring interns or kids like
“Hey read this and do coverage on it.”

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:56.520
They have experienced readers.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.830
When you have enough of those competitions
saying “I think this guy has got something.”

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:09.660
Or “I think this script is something else,
it’s a fresh voice,” what have you.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:14.509
I think there is some legitimacy to “This
person really does have some talent” because

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.660
you have some experienced third parties who
have an eye for talent, this is what we do

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:24.560
as producers and directors, we identify talent.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:32.110
And I think if you can excel at some of those
competitions, you are going to get those opportunities

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.780
to interface with agents or managers.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:42.870
If you can at least hit in a big competition
(the quarter-final level), I think you can

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:44.289
get agents or managers to read your script.

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:47.380
If you can get to the semi-final, they will
probably be calling you.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.730
And if you can get to the finals level, you
will probably get meetings.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:56.069
I mean if you are a finalist at Nichols, you
are going to be able to get meetings with

00:05:56.069 --> 00:06:00.289
William Morris and CAA because that is such
a limited pool of people compared to the number

00:06:00.289 --> 00:06:01.289
that apply.

00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:04.949
I mean literally thousand of thousands of
people apply and it gets down to literally

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:12.300
a small handful of people, so I think that’s
another way to get to agents and managers.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.220
So play out your own resources base and see
who you know that is already repped and see

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.410
if you can get in that way.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:23.110
And also competitions, I think that is two
ways to get to access to agents or managers.

